  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 AM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThis morning's 06Z RAP surface analysis shows the center of the \nOntario low sitting at 1006 mb, just west of James Bay. Meanwhile, a \npositively-tilted surface trough is draped from the Minnesota River \nValley toward the Nebraska Sandhills while ridging builds in to the \nUpper Great Lakes between the aforementioned two features. Per WPC \nanalysis, an occluded front attached to the Ontario low lies across \nthe eastern UP from approximately from Grand Marais toward Door \nCounty, WI. Those behind it have some relief from Tuesday's warm \nmuggy conditions, with temperatures in the 50s and 60s and dewpoints \ndepressions ranging from 1-7 degrees. In Schoolcraft, Delta, and \nLuce counties, however, temperatures tend to be in the high 60s to \n70s with dewpoint depressions from 0-4 degrees; the Newberry METAR \nis currently reporting thick fog.\n\nThe main story for today are the scattered showers which will make \ntheir way across the southern part of the UP this morning. As the \nPlains trough progresses east with zonal flow along with continued \nPacific moisture, PWAT increases to 1-1.25\" for most of the UP and \nto 1.5\" in Menominee County. Most of this moisture, however, is \nabove 700 mb; what precipitation falls out from the relatively weak \nforcing will have to survive through a stout dry layer to reach the \nsurface. Thus, anticipated QPF resides generally below a wetting \nrain (0.10\"), except for southern portions of Menominee County which \nmay see slightly higher accumulations. High temperatures today \nshould land in the high 70s to low 80s, which, although cooler than \nyesterday, is still 5-10F above climate normal. Given NW'ly surface \nflow, areas with onshore flow from Lake Superior may see lower high \ntemperatures.\n\nMuch of the remainder of the week will be dominated by upper level \nridging over the central CONUS and broad troughing over eastern \nCanada, leaving the Upper Great Lakes in a fairly benign northwest \nflow pattern between the two features. The influence of the ridge \nwill be enough to keep temperatures above seasonal norms across the \nthe UP through the period, with daytime highs staying in the mid to \nupper 70s and overnight lows generally in the mid to upper 50s \nthrough the weekend. Do not see any significant rainfall in the \nforecast beyond Wednesday, but a few disturbances embedded in the \nflow will at least keep some periodic shower chances around from \nThursday into the weekend.",
